On the twelfth day of my internship, I started learning 
more about the Bash shell and shell scripting. I read 
the introductory Bash sections from the Linux book and 
learned what a shell script is and why scripts are useful
for automating repetitive tasks.

I learned about the basic structure of a Bash script and 
the purpose of the shebang line, such as #!/bin/bash. 
I also studied how Bash scripts can be executed from 
the terminal and how executable permissions are related 
to running scripts.

In addition to the book, I started watching the Shell Tools 
and Scripting materials from The Missing Semester. 
These resources helped me understand the relationship 
between the commands I had been using in the terminal 
and the scripts that can be created using those commands.
